=== agent-deck connect: Any HTTP client (generic) (name "http", scopes read) ===
Token file: /home/me/.local/share/agent-deck/assistants/http.token (0600). Revoke: agent-deck connect revoke http
Needs `agent-deck web` running and reachable at http://127.0.0.1:8420.

--- 1. Config: your program's environment, then call the API ---
# In the bot's environment:
AGENTDECK_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8420
AGENTDECK_ASSISTANT_TOKEN=adk_GOLDENTOKEN

# The bot calls (JSON in, JSON out):
curl -fsS -H "Authorization: Bearer $AGENTDECK_ASSISTANT_TOKEN" "$AGENTDECK_URL/api/assistant/v1/sessions"
curl -fsS -H "Authorization: Bearer $AGENTDECK_ASSISTANT_TOKEN" "$AGENTDECK_URL/api/assistant/v1/sessions/SESSION_ID/output"
curl -fsS -H "Authorization: Bearer $AGENTDECK_ASSISTANT_TOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"message":"..."}' "$AGENTDECK_URL/api/assistant/v1/sessions/SESSION_ID/send"   # needs scope send

# Python:
# requests.get(os.environ["AGENTDECK_URL"] + "/api/assistant/v1/sessions",
#              headers={"Authorization": "Bearer " + os.environ["AGENTDECK_ASSISTANT_TOKEN"]}).json()

# All routes (Authorization: Bearer token on every call):
# GET  http://127.0.0.1:8420/api/assistant/v1/whoami
# GET  http://127.0.0.1:8420/api/assistant/v1/sessions[?group=G&status=S]
# GET  http://127.0.0.1:8420/api/assistant/v1/sessions/ID
# GET  http://127.0.0.1:8420/api/assistant/v1/sessions/ID/output
# POST http://127.0.0.1:8420/api/assistant/v1/sessions/ID/send     {"message": "..."}           (send)
# POST http://127.0.0.1:8420/api/assistant/v1/sessions/ID/approve  {"choice": "once"}          (approve)
# POST http://127.0.0.1:8420/api/assistant/v1/sessions             {"path","title","tool","message"}  (launch)

--- 2. Test ---
agent-deck connect test --name http --url http://127.0.0.1:8420

--- 3. Guide: keep this text in the system prompt of the model that drives your program ---
You are connected to agent-deck on my machine as "http" with scopes: read.
agent-deck is a terminal session manager for AI coding agents (Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, OpenCode and others): each session is one agent working in its own terminal. Through the agent-deck tools you can see those sessions and, when allowed, drive them.

Tools (MCP server "agentdeck"; the HTTP API at http://127.0.0.1:8420/api/assistant/v1 mirrors them):
- whoami: your name, scopes and limits
- list_sessions {group?, status?}: all sessions with id, title, tool, status, group, path (read)
- session_status {session}: one session's details (read)
- read_output {session}: the last answer of that session's agent (read)
- send_message {session, message}: type a message into a session (send)
- approve {session, choice?}: answer a visible approval prompt (approve)
- launch_session {path, title, tool?, message?}: start a new session (launch)

Rules:
1. Call list_sessions first and refer to sessions by id.
2. Read before acting: check session_status or read_output before you send or approve anything.
3. ASK THE USER and wait for a clear yes before send_message, approve or launch_session; show the exact text you will send.
4. Never stop, delete, restart or kill sessions. There is no tool for it; do not use a shell or any other way to do it.
5. Never read, print or change credentials, tokens or agent-deck's own files (~/.agent-deck, ~/.local/share/agent-deck).
6. If a tool answers "scope missing", do not work around it: tell the user the exact command from the answer (for example: agent-deck connect http --scopes read,send).
7. Keep reports short: one line per session (title, status, what it is waiting for).

To allow more, run: agent-deck connect http --scopes read,send,approve,launch (pick only the scopes you need; this rotates the token, stdio configs pick the new one up from the token file, HTTP configs need the new token)
=== end ===
